# The Orton effect, a landscape photographers' technique from slide-film days: sandwich a sharp exposure with an
# overexposed, out-of-focus one. The result keeps its detail but glows, with luminous, painterly colour. In Cierra
# the sandwich is a parallel(): both branches see the same picture and the result is their average.
#   crisp          branch 1, the sharp slide: more local contrast and texture
#   orton_bright   branch 2, the overexposed slide, a stop and a half over ...
#   orton_blur     ... and out of focus
#   parallel(...)  the sandwich: the average of the two (a 50 / 50 blend in ACEScct)
#   settle         the bright branch lifts the whole picture: bring the mids back down, keep the glow
#   pop            firmer contrast: the sandwich is soft
#   lush           greens richer and a little toward yellow (spring foliage), skies a touch richer
#   golden         warm late-day light in the highlights
# Use it for landscapes, gardens, travel and nature films; on faces it reads as soft focus.
cierra 0.1
name "Orton landscape"
about "A sharp and an overexposed, blurred copy averaged with parallel(): detail that glows; lush greens, warm light."
input aces2065_1
output aces2(display: rec1886)

node crisp = local_contrast(amount: 0.35, radius_px: 60)
  label "the sharp slide"
node orton_bright = exposure(stops: 1.5)
  label "the overexposed slide"
node orton_blur = blur(radius_px: 24)
  label "out of focus"
node settle = exposure(stops: -0.55)
  label "mids back where they were"
node pop = contrast(amount: 1.18, pivot: 0.38)
  label "the sandwich is soft: firm it up"
node lush = hue_vs_sat(points: [[206, 1.4, 45], [173, 1.2, 30], [350, 1.1, 40]])
  label "richer greens, yellows and blue sky"
node spring = hue_vs_hue(points: [[206, -10, 40]])
  label "greens a little toward yellow"
node golden = split_tone(highlight_hue: 155, highlight_amount: 0.02)
  label "warm light"

graph parallel(crisp, orton_bright -> orton_blur) -> settle -> pop -> lush -> spring -> golden
